    <body>
      <sec id="article-126819.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Trigonocephaly, a form of craniosynostosis, is characterized by the premature fusion of the metopic suture. This course outlines the clinical manifestations of this condition, including a triangular forehead, hypotelorism, lateral orbital retrusion, and compensatory parieto-occipital widening, which may lead to elevated intracranial pressure and neurodevelopmental delays. Participants will explore the clinical and radiographic features that distinguish trigonocephaly from benign metopic ridges, including imaging and anthropometric assessments.</p>
        <p>The activity examines the multifactorial etiology, including genetic mutations, intrauterine constraints, maternal metabolic factors, and environmental influences. Surgical management, encompassing endoscopic, open, and advanced cranial reconstruction techniques, will be reviewed, along with indications, timing, and potential complications.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance learners' competence in early recognition, diagnostic evaluation, surgical planning, long-term management, and the implementation of&#x000a0;an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing this condition,&#x000a0;ultimately improving patient outcomes, cranial development, and neurocognitive function in affected children.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the key features that distinguish trigonocephaly from benign metopic ridges in pediatric patients.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based imaging techniques to accurately assess the extent of metopic craniosynostosis.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Develop individualized perioperative management plans to minimize surgical complications in patients with&#x000a0;trigonocephaly.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement interprofessional team strategies for improving care coordination and&#x000a0;outcomes for patients with trigonocephaly.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=126819&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=126819">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Trigonocephaly is the premature closure of the metopic suture, preventing the frontal bones from growing laterally, resulting in a triangular forehead with an obvious or subtle&#x000a0;osseous midline vertical ridge. The term "trigonocephaly" was first coined by Welcker in 1862.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;The word metopic comes from the Greek&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">metopon</italic>, meaning "forehead".<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Premature fusion of the metopic suture is a type of craniosynostosis that results in a narrow forehead (see <bold>Image</bold>. Craniosynostosis). The position of the eyes is closer than usual (hypotelorism) with raised or arched eyebrows, and lateral orbital retrusion with associated bilateral frontotemporal narrowing and compensatory occipital-biparietal widening.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Trigonocephaly should be distinguished from a benign metopic ridge, where hypotelorism and frontotemporal narrowing are absent, and no other clinical features are present.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Computed tomography (CT) scans with 3-dimensional (3-D) reconstructions can be used to distinguish between the&#x000a0;2 conditions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Trigonocephaly is a mechanical issue where surgical intervention is the main treatment modality for these patients.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Trigonocephaly is a postneurulation defect&#x000a0;that results in premature fusion of the metopic suture, typically occurring before birth.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Normal closure of the metopic suture starts at 3&#x000a0;to 4 months of age and is completed at 8&#x000a0;to 9 months&#x000a0;in most individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;The closure begins at the nasion and extends superiorly before terminating at the anterior fontanelle.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Approximately 5.1% of the individuals have an open suture after&#x000a0;1 year of age. However, by&#x000a0;2 years of age, the suture is usually closed. 3-D CT scans have demonstrated that physiologic fusion of the metopic suture occurs between the third and nineteenth months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;Premature suture closure before the third month will cause trigonocephaly.</p>
        <p>The specific etiology of craniosynostosis is unknown.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Genetic abnormalities, signaling pathway alterations, environmental factors, metabolic disorders, and intrauterine constraints have been implicated as causes for craniosynostosis. The most notable genetic mutations recently identified in nonsyndromic metopic synostosis are&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">FREM1</italic>&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">SMAD6</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;Intrauterine constraint is defined as a condition where fetal movements are significantly reduced where the fetus cannot move freely and if prolonged, craniofacial anomalies will result.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Some reports have associated the maternal use of valproic acid with metopic craniosynostosis, although no association was confirmed in a recent meta-analysis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Rare nonsyndromic cases have been described in which there is the fusion of both the metopic and the sagittal sutures, producing significant behavioral, cognitive, and motor&#x000a0;impairment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;These patients require a combined surgical approach to treat both sutures. Maternal thyroid dysfunction can contribute to metopic synostosis, which may be followed by sagittal and coronal synostosis in children. Folic acid deficiency before pregnancy has also been&#x000a0;suggested as a causative factor for trigonocephaly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The number of children born&#x000a0;worldwide with craniosynostosis in&#x000a0;2019 was estimated to be 84,665, which included 72,857 with nonsyndromic craniosynostosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;The overall incidence of all types of craniosynostosis is approximately 0.6&#x000a0;per 1000 live births. Metopic craniosynostosis comprises 10% to 31% of all isolated craniosynostosis and is more commonly seen in males.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;The incidence of metopic craniosynostosis is approximately 1&#x000a0;per 5000&#x000a0;to 15,000 live births.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;The prevalence of metopic craniosynostosis has been increasing over the years in both Europe and the United States.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Metopic synostosis is now the second most common type of craniosynostosis, after scaphocephaly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;About three-quarters of the patients are male, with a&#x000a0;male-to-female ratio of 3.3:1.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Nonsyndromic craniosynostosis accounts for approximately 85% of all craniosynostosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;In more than 95% of cases, craniosynostosis is sporadic.&#x000a0;Trigonocephaly is usually nonsyndromic but can be associated with a syndrome in some cases. In a study evaluating the role of ethnicity in the development of craniosynostosis, white race and African American parents were the principal ethnic groups identified.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Many syndromic craniosynostosis patients have cognitive disabilities. This is more prominent in metopic craniosynostosis than in any other single-suture craniosynostosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;About one-fourth of the patients with single-suture craniosynostosis have other organ-associated anomalies.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Approximately 6% to 10% of patients with trigonocephaly demonstrate genetic alterations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;Metopic&#x000a0;craniosynostosis has been associated with several&#x000a0;chromosomal abnormalities that include the deletion of chromosome 11q24, 7p, 3q, 9p, 13q, 12pter, 22q11, trisomy of 9p or 18, duplication of 15q25, and <italic toggle="yes">ERF</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">SMAD6</italic> gene mutations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;Familial cases occur in approximately 5% of cases, with an&#x000a0;8% frequency in twins.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r37">[37]</xref></p>
        <p>Premature fusion of the metopic suture restricts lateral growth of the frontal bones and shortens the anterior cranial fossa.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r10">[10]</xref> Suture fusion produces a midline vertical bony ridge along the forehead. Restricted lateral expansion of the frontal bones results in decreased interorbital distance, causing the orbits to appear closer together and producing bitemporal narrowing. Closure of the anterior fontanelle occurs in approximately half of affected cases.</p>
        <p>Elevated intracranial pressure (ICP) occurs in approximately 8% to 33% of patients with metopic craniosynostosis and can contribute to neurodevelopmental delays.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r42">[42]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r43">[43]</xref> One series reported elevated ICP in 78% of patients during surgery for trigonocephaly; however, most patients in that cohort were older, with a mean age of 5 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r44">[44]</xref> Such observations indicate that uncorrected metopic craniosynostosis can significantly impair language development, motor function, and behavior. Compensatory growth of the remaining cranial sutures maintains the cephalic index within normal limits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r46">[46]</xref> The posterior skull undergoes sagittal and transverse expansion, accompanied by vertical and sagittal growth of the upper face.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r45">[45]</xref> Continued compensatory expansion of unfused sutures produces the widened parieto-occipital skull appearance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r47">[47]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Physical examination shows a visible midline vertical forehead bony ridge leading to a deformity resembling the keel of a ship. Hypotelorism is frequently present, and patients have bitemporal narrowing with compensatory biparietal widening. The&#x000a0;earlier that the suture closes, the more severe the skull deformity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r47">[47]</xref></p>
        <p>Craniosynostosis cases can be classified as mild, moderate, and severe depending on the angulation of the frontal bones.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;Fundoscopy can be used to detect elevated ICP in patients with craniosynostosis, where papilledema is more commonly found in older children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r49">[49]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The diagnosis of trigonocephaly is primarily clinical, with radiographic studies used to confirm the diagnosis. The most obvious clinical sign present in the diagnosis is physical palpation of the&#x000a0;anterior midsagittal vertical bony ridge over the forehead.&#x000a0;When viewed from the vertex or by a "birds-eye" view, the triangular shape of the head is&#x000a0;characteristic of this condition.</p>
        <p>Craniofacial anomalies, eg, hypotelorism and temporal narrowing with parietal widening, are usually sufficient to make an accurate diagnosis of craniosynostosis. These additional features can help to differentiate craniosynostosis from a benign&#x000a0;metopic ridge.</p>
        <p>A head CT scan with 3-D reconstructions is performed to confirm the diagnosis of trigonocephaly and plan for surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r50">[50]</xref>&#x000a0;The CT scan will demonstrate triangular frontal bones with orbits closer than normal and anterior displacement of the coronal sutures. Widening of the posterior parietal regions, pterional constriction, and flattening of the supraorbital ridges with lateral orbital hypoplasia can also be identified. Several measurements can be made to help classify the severity of synostosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r51">[51]</xref> Sisti et al provide a comprehensive review of anthropometric measurements for patients with trigonocephaly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r52">[52]</xref></p>
        <p>Prenatal head ultrasound can be used during&#x000a0;the second and third trimesters to diagnose craniosynostosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r53">[53]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r54">[54]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r55">[55]</xref>&#x000a0;Ultrasound can also be used in infants between the ages of 8&#x000a0;and 12 months to&#x000a0;avoid&#x000a0;radiation exposure and the need for sedation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r56">[56]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r57">[57]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r58">[58]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r59">[59]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r60">[60]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Radiographic Findings Associated with Metopic Synostosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>The presence of&#x000a0;3 or more specific findings is diagnostic for metopic synostosis in 96% of patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r61">[61]</xref>&#x000a0;These findings are as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p>Omega sign: the prematurely fused metopic suture forms an "omega" shaped invagination intracranially</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Interorbital narrowing: decreased distance between the medial orbital walls</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Upper orbital narrowing: the shape of the upper orbit is narrower than usual</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The frontal bone tangent intersects the orbital midline or more medially when viewed from above</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Posteriorly displaced lateral frontal bone</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Straight lateral frontal bone</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>"Pulled" anterior fontanelle: anterior fontanelle is partially open down to the metopic suture</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Upsloping lateral orbital rim: the superior orbital rim is oriented more vertically than horizontally</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Early operative intervention is recommended to maximize the brain's ability to expand and achieve a normal skull configuration. The goals of surgery are to remove the bony ridge of the metopic suture, advance both orbits and the frontal bones, achieve a rounder forehead contour, and prevent a psychosocial impact and neurodevelopmental delay (see <bold>Image</bold>. Interfrontal Suture).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;Advancing the orbits and frontal bones will&#x000a0;improve the lateral and superior orbital rim projection,&#x000a0;expand the&#x000a0;lateral and superior frontal bones, and correct the pterional and frontozygomatic restriction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Endoscopic Surgery</bold>
</p>
        <p>This technique is best used in infants younger than 3 to 4 months of age because of the pliability of the skull. A small incision is made behind the hairline towards the metopic suture. A subgaleal plane is developed from the anterior fontanelle to the nasofrontal suture. A single burr hole is made, and a 30-degree endoscope is used to dissect the dura and the overlying stenosed suture. An osteotomy is made between the anterior fontanelle and the nasion using&#x000a0;scissors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r63">[63]</xref>&#x000a0;A helmet is prepared and placed on the patient on the fifth postoperative day. The helmet is used for the next 10&#x000a0;to 12 months&#x000a0;to direct cranial growth by&#x000a0;allowing cranial expansion. Endoscopic surgery significantly reduces blood loss, operative time, length of stay, and the transfusion rate compared to open techniques.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r64">[64]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r65">[65]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r66">[66]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r68">[68]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Open Surgery Approaches</bold>
</p>
        <p>Open surgery is usually performed before age 1 year (typically at 9 to 12 months). A less radical procedure is used when the primary goal is to remove the midline bony ridge, and orbital rim advancement is not needed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r69">[69]</xref>&#x000a0;Drilling&#x000a0;of the prominent metopic suture suffices. If a temporalis hollow is present, the temporalis muscle can be partially split and moved to a more anterior position. A zigzag or wavy coronal incision is used to preserve the superficial temporal arteries and to maintain a robust blood supply. Dissection of the scalp flaps is performed above the periosteal plane to reduce bleeding.</p>
        <p>Open surgery requires a larger incision and is usually associated with a longer operative time and length of stay.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r70">[70]</xref> Blood transfusions are almost always required. All of these&#x000a0;drawbacks will increase the overall healthcare costs. In those cases where cranial reconstruction is required, a bifrontal craniotomy is performed to reconstruct the anterior cranial vault, including the lateral portions of the sphenoid wings, to allow for brain expansion. The bifrontal bone flap undergoes radial osteotomies to reshape the bone. The midline ridge requires shaping with&#x000a0;the drill. To perform the fronto-orbital advancement, the periosteum above the supraorbital rim is opened before the bilateral orbital rim osteotomy, and advancement is performed.</p>
        <p>Orbital osteotomies are performed using a reciprocating bone saw. A free supraorbital bone is created and reshaped into a more convex configuration, especially along the superolateral orbital rim. This is&#x000a0;performed with several&#x000a0;closed wedge osteotomies along the&#x000a0;free supraorbital bone bar. If the hypoteloric deformity is severe, an interpositional split-thickness cranial bone graft is added. The orbital rim is moved forward, and fixation plates are placed to maintain the advancement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r71">[71]</xref></p>
        <p>Alternatively, absorbable sutures can be used. Using sutures and avoiding plates and screws reduces surgery costs and allows for natural reshaping.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;The temporal bone is split and advanced to correct the abnormal inward angulation after the lateral orbital rims are advanced.&#x000a0;For those patients operated on at an early age, overcorrection&#x000a0;by approximately 1.5 cm is recommended to compensate for the&#x000a0;impaired bifrontal growth.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r72">[72]</xref>&#x000a0;Using preoperative erythropoietin and ferrous sulfate with the addition of perioperative tranexamic acid reduces blood transfusions in patients with metopic craniosynostosis undergoing craniofacial reconstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r73">[73]</xref></p>
        <p>An alternative approach when reconstructing the orbits is to remodel the orbital rim in situ to preserve the attachment to the zygoma and incline the cephalic portion of the supraorbital rim anteriorly. The fixation is&#x000a0;performed using resorbable fixation plates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r74">[74]</xref> The orbital rim will advance with brain growth. Another alternative is the in situ bandeau approach, in which the supraorbital bandeau is not separated at the central point at the nasion to maintain its blood supply.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;Partial osteotomies are made to facilitate the bending of the bandeau. Using bone benders, the bandeau is flattened&#x000a0;to bring forward the supraorbital rims and the lateral bone margins. The lateral margins are bent back with greenstick&#x000a0;fractures to align with the temporal bones, which are repositioned outward and then fixed to the bandeau with resorbable plates. In another alternative procedure, the supraorbital bandeau is left in situ at the temporal bones, split at the midline using a hinge technique, and an interpositional bone graft is placed in the nasofrontal area.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r75">[75]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Lille&#x02019;s technique</bold>
</p>
        <p>Lille's technique involves resection of the metopic suture, followed by construction of a large frontal panel stabilized with absorbable plates. Fronto-orbital advancement, parietal craniectomies, and pterional resection accompany this. This technique facilitates the optimization of cranial expansion and permits future cranial growth.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r76">[76]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Di Rocco&#x000a0;technique</bold>
</p>
        <p>The Di Rocco technique helps to control bleeding over the glabella and ophryon. This technique follows the principle of direct visualization of emissary metopic veins and the superior sagittal sinus, thereby having better control of hemostasis before removing the last piece of the frontal bone, achieved by manipulating the design of the frontal craniotomy so that a triangle of bone based above the glabella at the site of the osteotomy of the bandeau is conserved.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r77">[77]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>&#x0201c;Hypercorrection&#x0201d; method</bold>
</p>
        <p>This technique, proposed by Bennett et al, helps achieve better cosmetic results and reduce relapse and the need for revision surgery. The method involves a fronto-orbital advancement of 2.5 to 3.5 cm and concomitant hyperexpansion of the bitemporal projection. Despite this, some patients may need additional corrections, eg, lateral orbital recession and bitemporal constriction, which have been reported rarely following this procedure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r78">[78]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r79">[79]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Conservative Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>If the deformity is mild or there is only a bony ridge without hypotelorism, a more conservative approach can be used, in which the child is followed until at least 12 months of age to assess and&#x000a0;corroborate&#x000a0;adequate cranial growth. Clinical evaluations and family discussions regarding neuropsychological development are engaged during this time.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;During the first years of life, the child's development is periodically evaluated to ascertain that no language, motor, or behavior problems develop.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The most common differential diagnosis of trigonocephaly is a metopic ridge, which is physiological and does not need surgery. Trigonocephaly presents at a younger age, and the appearance of the lateral&#x000a0;frontal bone and lateral orbit can help distinguish it from a pathological condition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>The single metopic suture craniosynostosis must be differentiated from the following syndromic craniosynostoses, which most commonly involve the coronal suture and produce brachycephaly:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Apert syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Crouzon&#x000a0;syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Muenke&#x000a0;syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pfeiffer syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Saethre-Chotzen&#x000a0;syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Craniofrontonasal syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Carpenter&#x000a0;syndrome&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r80">[80]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Secondary synostosis can be caused by several conditions, but often involves multiple sutures, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyperthyroidism</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypercalcemia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypophosphatemia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rickets</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sickle cell anemia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Thalassemia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Polycythemia vera</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Microcephaly</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prematurity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Teratogens (phenytoin, valproic acid, retinoic acid, aminopterin)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r81">[81]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r82">[82]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Surgical correction produces satisfactory cosmetic outcomes in most patients. At 2 years of age, comparisons between endoscopic strip craniectomy followed by helmet therapy and open skull reconstruction show excellent head-shape outcomes with both approaches.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r83">[83]</xref> One study reported superior long-term appearance and improved perioperative outcomes with endoscopic strip craniectomy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r84">[84]</xref> Most surgically treated patients achieve academic performance near the national mean and demonstrate above-average intelligence quotient scores. However, neurocognitive performance correlates with the severity of craniosynostosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r85">[85]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r86">[86]</xref>&#x000a0;Furthermore, greater severity of preoperative phenotypes also correlates with less favorable aesthetic outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r87">[87]</xref> Mortality associated with all forms of craniosynostosis remains well below 1%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r37">[37]</xref></p>
        <p>Untreated patients demonstrate deficits in cognitive functioning compared with healthy controls.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r88">[88]</xref> Severe untreated metopic craniosynostosis has been associated with reduced auditory processing of language stimuli within the frontal cortex, suggesting that orbitofrontal deformity adversely affects frontal cortical function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>Children with metopic craniosynostosis, whether treated or untreated, frequently demonstrate lower performance in general cognition, motor functioning, attention, verbal and visuospatial abilities, and behavior compared with healthy peers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r89">[89]</xref> Intellectual disabilities and behavioral disorders may develop or persist despite surgical correction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r89">[89]</xref> Research indicates that nearly a third of patients with metopic craniosynostosis experience developmental delays.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r90">[90]</xref> Behavioral, cognitive, and psychological difficulties in these patients often require continued support and long-term monitoring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r89">[89]</xref> Ongoing neurodevelopmental and ophthalmologic follow-up remains recommended for patients with complex craniosynostosis, even after successful cranial remodeling.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Surgical complications after metopic craniosynostosis correction are relatively few and usually minor.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r91">[91]</xref>&#x000a0;These include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Excessive blood loss</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dural tears</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Meningitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Stroke</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pseudomeningocele</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyperthermia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hypoactivity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Local wound infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Epidural abscess</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Subcutaneous hematoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Epidural hematoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Subdural hematoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Air embolus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Incomplete correction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Temporal hollowing</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Stretched scar/keloid</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Reoperation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Contact dermatitis from helmet use</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Scalp abrasion from helmet use</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Despite surgical correction, some patients may still present with elevated ICP, estimated to occur in 3% of cases, with an average time of 57 months after the initial surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r92">[92]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r93">[93]</xref>&#x000a0;This is corrected with a&#x000a0;subsequent calvarial expansion surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Late Complications</bold>
</p>
        <p>Some patients need additional surgery to fill bone defects later in life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r92">[92]</xref>&#x000a0;Contour irregularities are evident in the frontal and temporal regions. This may require further treatment in the form of lipoinjection if the condition is affecting the child's academic performance and personality development, or a recontouring procedure using a hydroxyapatite onlay&#x000a0;after the age of 14 to 16 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r94">[94]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;For those with long-term sequelae, using cutting guides to facilitate scheduled complex reconstruction with autologous bone can be useful.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r95">[95]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s12" sec-type="Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care">
        <title>Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care</title>
        <p>Patients are sent to the pediatric intensive care unit overnight to closely monitor vital signs and evaluate the necessity of additional blood transfusions. The drain, if one is placed, is removed the next day. If stable, the patient is sent to the ward for another day. Upon discharge, the patient is followed up at the clinic for wound inspection and suture removal. The head shape is followed at&#x000a0;3 months,&#x000a0;6 months, and then annually with&#x000a0;photography and&#x000a0;anthropometric measurements. The patients are also closely evaluated for&#x000a0;behavioral, cognitive, and psychological problems.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s13" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>The following consultations are required:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pediatric neurosurgeon</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Plastic surgeon</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Craniofacial surgeon</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pediatric intensivist</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pediatric anesthesiologist</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ophthalmologist</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Geneticist</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Surgical correction of trigonocephaly is strongly supported before 1 year of age because earlier intervention improves cosmetic outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r96">[96]</xref> Management of mild trigonocephaly continues to generate debate. A survey distributed to 102 craniofacial surgeons revealed significant variation in opinions regarding the treatment of mild nonsyndromic metopic craniosynostosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r97">[97]</xref> Despite differing views in mild cases, consensus exists for surgical intervention in moderate and severe presentations. Most craniofacial surgeons favor open surgical techniques, although the specific operative approach differs among practitioners. Optimal timing for orbital advancement remains a topic of discussion, yet most surgeons perform this procedure between 6 and 10 months of age.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r97">[97]</xref></p>
        <p>Screening for&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">SMAD6</italic>&#x000a0;mutations receives a recommendation in patients with nonsyndromic metopic craniosynostosis because detection indicates an elevated risk of recurrence in a subsequent pregnancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-126819.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Identification of this mutation warrants referral for genetic counseling.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Trigonocephaly, resulting from premature fusion of the metopic suture, produces a triangular forehead, hypotelorism, bitemporal narrowing, and compensatory parieto-occipital widening. This craniosynostosis can elevate intracranial pressure and impair neurodevelopment if not identified and managed early. Clinical evaluation combines careful physical examination, palpation of the midsagittal forehead ridge, and radiographic confirmation with 3-dimensional CT or ultrasound in younger infants. Management strategies depend on severity and age at diagnosis and include endoscopic suturectomy, open cranial vault reconstruction, or hybrid approaches to restore normal cranial shape, optimize orbital positioning, and reduce neurodevelopmental and psychosocial sequelae. Early recognition and timely intervention remain essential to prevent long-term functional deficits.</p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration is critical for optimizing outcomes in trigonocephaly. Pediatric neurosurgeons, plastic and craniofacial surgeons, pediatric anesthesiologists, and intensivists coordinate surgical evaluation and operative care using integrated, evidence-based pathways. Primary care clinicians facilitate early referral, while nurses support patient assessment, education, and care coordination. Pharmacists assist with perioperative management, and allied health professionals monitor developmental progress. Open communication, shared decision-making with families, and structured follow-up enhance safety, minimize complications, and ensure comprehensive, patient-centered care.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-126819.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
